x_K'(D) = (D**2/2 + D*(2*K*gamma - 2*K - 2*n + 1)/2 + K**2*gamma**2/2 - K**2*gamma + K**2/2 - K*gamma*n + K*gamma/2 + K*n - K/2 - n/2 + 1/12)/n**2

Two exact critical-point roots (function of K, n, gamma):
  D* = -K*gamma + K + n - sqrt(36*n**2 + 3)/6 - 1/2
  D* = -K*gamma + K + n + sqrt(36*n**2 + 3)/6 - 1/2

==============================================================================
Which root is O(K) (i.e. potentially inside [-gamma*K,(1-gamma)*K])
and which is O(n) (far outside the support for large n)?
==============================================================================
Root 0: D*/K -> sqrt(2)*(-sqrt(2)*gamma*sqrt(-1/(gamma - 2)) + sqrt(2)*sqrt(-1/(gamma - 2)))/(2*sqrt(-1/(gamma - 2))) as n->infty (gamma symbolic)
Root 1: D*/K -> oo*sign(1/sqrt(-1/(gamma - 2))) as n->infty (gamma symbolic)

==============================================================================
Evaluate the 'small' root (the one that stays O(K)) at several gamma,
check numerically whether it falls inside (D_min,D_max) = 
(-gamma*K, (1-gamma)*K), and if so, compare x_K there to the two
endpoint values -- at ASTRONOMICALLY large, representative finite n
(mpmath, high precision, exact c_i(K,n,gamma) -- no leading-order
shortcut in this numeric check).
==============================================================================
